1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,970
The brush uncovers a boundary stone.

2
00:00:02,970 --> 00:00:06,327
Somebody walked the edge of a village, agreed where it ran,

3
00:00:06,327 --> 00:00:11,102
and set stones into the ground so the agreement would outlast the memory of it.

4
00:00:17,167 --> 00:00:22,077
A boundary stone is a small object with a large implication: somebody now owns what is inside it,

5
00:00:22,077 --> 00:00:26,336
and the question of who has been one of the great arguments in Indian history.

6
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:37,902
Almost entirely inscriptions — thousands of copper plates and stone inscriptions recording grants, from the early centuries CE onwards.

7
00:00:37,902 --> 00:00:41,581
Plus law books and other texts, which supply the vocabulary.

8
00:00:41,581 --> 00:00:48,876
This episode is the best example in the series of an argument conducted almost entirely from one class of document.

9
00:00:51,967 --> 00:00:57,188
Start with what a grant actually says, because the clauses are the evidence.

10
00:00:57,188 --> 00:00:59,019
The donor, usually a king.

11
00:00:59,019 --> 00:01:03,495
The recipient — a Brahmin, a temple, a monastery, or an official.

12
00:01:03,495 --> 00:01:07,864
The village or land granted, with its boundaries described.

13
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:16,562
The immunities: freedom from specified taxes, and often the right to collect them instead.

14
00:01:16,562 --> 00:01:20,749
Sometimes the right to administer, to judge, even to keep fines.

15
00:01:20,749 --> 00:01:25,043
And at the end, frequently, a curse on anyone who revokes it.

16
00:01:27,367 --> 00:01:28,419
Now the argument.

17
00:01:28,419 --> 00:01:34,559
If a king grants away the revenue of a village in perpetuity, he has permanently reduced his own income.

18
00:01:34,559 --> 00:01:39,245
If he grants the right to administer it, he has reduced his own authority.

19
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:46,882
Do that a thousand times over several centuries and you have a state that no longer collects from, or governs,

20
00:01:46,882 --> 00:01:53,417
large parts of its own territory — and a layer of intermediaries between the king and the peasant who did not exist before.

21
00:01:56,600 --> 00:02:02,663
The historians who call this Indian feudalism, R. S. Sharma foremost among them, build a wider case.

22
00:02:02,663 --> 00:02:09,746
They argue that long-distance trade declined after the Gupta period, that fewer coins were struck, that towns shrank,

23
00:02:09,746 --> 00:02:16,528
that villages became self-sufficient, and that cultivators became tied to the land under their new masters.

24
00:02:18,967 --> 00:02:25,914
On this reading the whole early medieval period is a feudal order, comparable in structure if not in detail to medieval Europe.

25
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,020
The critics answer point by point.

26
00:02:31,020 --> 00:02:37,773
Coin finds and port activity suggest trade continued, with its routes and its goods changing rather than collapsing.

27
00:02:37,773 --> 00:02:40,948
Temple towns grew in the south in exactly this period.

28
00:02:40,948 --> 00:02:43,268
Merchant guilds remained powerful.

29
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:56,745
And they argue the European template imports the wrong questions: what the evidence shows is the formation of regional states with their own institutions — the integrative polity model,

30
00:02:56,745 --> 00:03:01,162
and in the far south the segmentary state model we met in the Chola episode.

31
00:03:04,267 --> 00:03:08,776
This is the single biggest debate in early Indian history, and it is not settled.

32
00:03:08,776 --> 00:03:11,086
You are not expected to settle it either.

33
00:03:11,086 --> 00:03:15,265
You are expected to know what a land grant contains, what each side argues,

34
00:03:15,265 --> 00:03:20,680
which evidence each relies on, and to notice that both sides are reading the same documents.

35
00:03:26,333 --> 00:03:33,811
A good answer names the historians, sets out both cases, and takes a position with reasons rather than pretending there is a consensus.

36
00:03:37,233 --> 00:03:39,893
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

37
00:03:39,893 --> 00:03:45,276
Grant, intermediary, weaker state — that is the feudalism argument in three words.

38
00:03:45,276 --> 00:03:48,235
The counter-argument disputes the third.

39
00:03:52,633 --> 00:03:53,388
Dig Quiz.

40
00:03:53,388 --> 00:03:54,822
Pause and name it.

41
00:03:54,822 --> 00:03:59,953
First: the clause in a grant freeing the land from specified taxes.

42
00:03:59,953 --> 00:04:01,234
An immunity.

43
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:10,339
Second: the term for a village granted to Brahmins, whose assembly we met in the Chola episode.

44
00:04:10,339 --> 00:04:13,299
A brahmadeya — agrahara is the other term.

45
00:04:15,433 --> 00:04:21,471
Third: the kind of evidence critics of the feudalism thesis use to argue that trade did not collapse.

46
00:04:21,471 --> 00:04:23,547
Coin finds and port activity.

47
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:29,428
In Mains, this debate comes up directly.

48
00:04:29,428 --> 00:04:35,242
Describe the grants, give both cases with their historians, and commit, with reasons.

49
00:04:38,333 --> 00:04:44,911
A boundary stone takes its place in the gallery, a small object that two generations of historians have argued over.

50
00:04:47,133 --> 00:04:52,117
Next gallery: the two kinds of evidence this whole series has leaned on hardest.

51
00:04:52,117 --> 00:04:55,924
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

